miniAMR, block-structured adaptive mesh refinement, distributed decomposition.

Same physics and the same problem instance as the sibling port in ../intel: the
7- or 27-point stencil over num_vars variables, the six-face halo with quarter
faces at a refinement boundary, the object sweep that drives refinement, the
8-way split and octet join, and the same three printed checksums -- the
`Weighted Mesh Checksum` this port prints is the other one's, digit for digit,
at the same arguments.  What differs is who builds the work:

  - a stage of a block is ONE task.  It takes in the strips its neighbours
    wrote at the previous stage, reflects the faces that are the edge of the
    mesh, runs the stencil over every variable, takes its checksum point where
    the stage is one, and writes the strips the neighbours read next.  The
    published port instead re-creates the block as a fresh EDT at every point
    where it must wait, and serialises the three halo axes because each is such
    a point;

  - a strip datablock always holds a whole face, which is also exactly four
    quarter faces, so it is created once for a block's lifetime and never
    resized however the mesh around it moves.  Each face keeps two generations:
    a stage reads the one the previous stage wrote and writes the other, so no
    strip has a reader and a writer in the same stage;

  - nothing is labeled.  A block finds the strip that feeds each face through
    the epoch's directory, so no halo channel is homed at an index unrelated to
    either endpoint;

  - every phase's fan-out is a spawn tree and every phase's fan-in a join tree,
    both indexed by rank, so a phase is emitted by as many tasks as the machine
    has room for and no task ever holds a dependence per block.  That is what
    removes the published port's cap on the block grid;

  - the checksum reduces over the block forest: an interior node adds its eight
    children in prong order, the base blocks are concatenated in mesh order,
    and one final task adds them left to right.  That is the published grouping,
    so the reduction is bitwise reproducible and independent of the node count;

  - the mesh of each epoch is a directory one task derives from the previous
    epoch and the object trajectory.  The published refinement decision reads a
    block's level and its integer centre and never a cell value, so the intent
    cascade, the level-difference agreement, the sibling unanimity and the
    coarsening wave are pure topology and need no distributed protocol.  What
    stays distributed is the part that moves data: one task per block of the new
    mesh, upsampling from its ancestor, summing its eight children, or keeping
    what it had, and then packing its strips;

  - blocks are mapped onto a three-dimensional rank grid by position, through a
    map that is level-consistent, so a block, its parent and its children share
    a rank and a refinement moves no payload across the fabric.

  --width  blocks emitted by one spawn-tree leaf (default 8); a leaf never
           crosses a rank, so a phase's issue frontier is the sum over ranks of
           ceil(that rank's blocks / width).

Two flags of the published command line are accepted and refused rather than
silently ignored: --code other than 0 (only minimal sends is implemented, as in
the published port) and a non-zero --plot_freq (this program writes no plot
files).  Bitwise identity with the published port is claimed for the 7-point
stencil; the 27-point form reads shell cells that no exchange ever writes, and
the published port warns that its answers diverge under refinement for that
reason.

Build with the parent project; sources: amr_dist.c command.c fan.c mesh.c
physics.c.
